+# 1. Visual Design Overview
+
+## 1.1 Product Context
+
+NovaTech Electronics is a small electronics/gadgets retailer. The application is used by an Administrator (owner/manager) and Inventory/Store Staff, on a single store PC, dozens of times a day, to track products, suppliers, customers, and stock movements. It is a C#/.NET WinForms desktop application (SRS §2, §7).
+
+## 1.2 Visual Personality
+
+**Professional, calm, trustworthy, efficient.** This is a piece of equipment the store relies on, not a marketing surface. It should feel like a well-organized ledger — understated, orderly, quick to scan — not a consumer dashboard or a student demo.
+
+Five words that should describe every screen: **modern, clean, trustworthy, calm, organized.**
+
+## 1.3 Design Principles (in priority order)
+
+1. **Information hierarchy** — the most important fact on any screen (stock status, quantity, total) is the most visually prominent thing on it.
+2. **Readability** — dense grids stay legible at native desktop resolution without zooming.
+3. **Consistency** — one Add/Edit pattern, one list pattern, one dialog pattern, one status-badge treatment, reused everywhere (per Phase 2 §6.1–6.11).
+4. **Efficient workflows** — the highest-frequency tasks (Stock-In, Stock-Out, product search) are visually the fastest to reach and complete.
+5. **Clear feedback** — every Save, error, and blocked action tells the user exactly what happened and what to do next.
+6. **Error prevention** — live validation (e.g., Stock-Out quantity vs. available stock) catches mistakes before Save, not just after.
+7. **Accessibility** — full keyboard operability, AA contrast, never color-alone meaning (detailed in `07-Accessibility.md`).
+8. **Desktop usability** — realistic for a store PC: fixed-width sidebar, anchored/docked grids, modal dialogs, standard Windows print.
+
+## 1.4 What This Design Avoids
+
+Per the approved brief (Phase 2 §10) and the visual-design brief for this phase:
+
+- Gradients, glassmorphism, drop shadows, glow effects
+- Animation-heavy transitions
+- Large decorative illustrations or hero graphics
+- Mobile-style UI (bottom nav bars, swipe gestures, card-stack feeds, pill-everything rounding)
+- Dark mode (not in MVP scope)
+- Any visual effect a standard WinForms control set (TextBox, Button, Label, ComboBox, DataGridView, DateTimePicker, CheckBox, Panel/GroupBox, MenuStrip/ToolStrip, StatusStrip, MessageBox) cannot realistically reproduce
+- Features, screens, or interactions not present in the approved Phase 1/2 specification
+
+## 1.5 Relationship to Phase 2 Design System
+
+Phase 2 §7 (`product-requirements/07-Design-System.md`) and §10 (Claude Visual Design Brief) already fixed the core visual language: color palette, typography, spacing scale, radius, and component style. This package does not re-litigate those decisions — `02-Design-Tokens.md` restates them in a single machine-readable reference, and every subsequent file (component states, shell, 20 screens, dialogs) is the detailed expansion of that already-approved language, not a new one. Delegation Management (SCR-020, added by SRS v1.1) reuses this same language exactly — no new colors, type sizes, or component idioms were introduced for it (see `03-Component-Library.md` §3.12, `05-Screen-Designs.md` §5.15).
+
+## 1.6 Design Target Statement
+
+> A store owner or staff member should be able to look at any screen in this application for the first time and immediately understand: where they are, what the most important number on the screen means, and what they're allowed to do next — without training, without a tooltip tour, and without needing to distinguish "is this button real or just decoration."
